; An occurrence marker on the single entry of a single-entry group-choice ARM
; (`t = [ x: uint // ? kv ]`) is REFUSED gracefully. A one-entry arm registers no record: the
; entry's TYPE goes straight into the enum variant, and a variant holds exactly one value, so there
; is nowhere for a repetition count to live and the marker was read by nothing at all. `? kv`,
; `* kv`, `+ kv` and `2*3 kv` each generated output BYTE-IDENTICAL to the unmarked `// kv`, at exit
; 0 — and the codec that output carries then rejects the counts the spec admits: the empty encoding
; a `?` / `*` / `0*n` arm allows comes back as `No variant matched … Definite length mismatch:
; found 0`, and (in an ARRAY) every 2-or-more encoding a `*` / `+` / `n*m` arm allows fails the
; same way. The defect is not group-specific — `[ x: uint // ? a: tstr ]` was byte-identical to its
; unmarked twin too — so the guard reads the ENTRY's occurrence and covers a plain group, an alias
; to one, a tagged one, and a plain keyed or bare member alike, in BOTH representations.
; What it does NOT cover is where dropping the marker is SOUND, which is not this guard's judgment
; to make twice: it asks `inline_group_occurrence_flattens`, the same predicate the inline-group
; splice asks. In a MAP that predicate carves out every lower-bound-≥1 marker (`+`, `2*3`, `2*`),
; because unique map keys mean a second repetition of a fixed-key alternative would duplicate its
; keys — so those admit count 1 and nothing else, dropping them is the HONORED semantics, and
; `{ x: uint // + kv }` keeps emitting the unmarked arm's bytes exactly (pinned as byte identity,
; not as "it generates"). Refusing them would have removed correct surface and would have had to
; claim a 2-or-more encoding a map does not have, which is why the message is rep-scoped: the array
; text names `?` / `*` / `+` / `n*m`, the map text only the zero-permitting `?` / `*` / `0*n` /
; `*n`.
; Honoring the marker is not a guard's work: a zero-case variant has to be TELLABLE on the wire,
; which means the sibling arms' own length checks must exclude the empty form — the occurrence/
; bounds program's scope (the queue's "unify non-final optional/repeated array decoding").
; The refusal is honest because the remedy it names is verified to generate: a TYPE choice over one
; named rule per count — `xarr = [x: uint]`, `kvarr = [kv]`, `empty = []`, `t = xarr / kvarr /
; empty` for `?`, with `kvs = [* kv]` in place of `kvarr` for `*`. The named-array WRAPPER
; (`w = [kv]` referenced from the arm) is deliberately NOT that remedy: it nests the group in an
; array of its own and so cannot express the empty case at all.
; Three deliberate non-firings: an absent marker and the pedantic `1*1` already mean exactly once in
; either rep, so dropping them narrows nothing (the same carve-out the array record-field loop's
; `narrows` guard makes); the map-side collapse above; and an inline-group entry
; (`? (a: uint, b: uint)`) is already refused in entry position for EVERY marker including none, so
; it keeps that message — one message per problem.
; Message identity, the per-marker × per-rep matrix, the profile independence and the neighbouring
; guards that must not be shadowed are pinned by
; `occurrence_on_single_entry_group_choice_arm_rejects_gracefully`.
kv = (a: uint, b: uint)
t = [ x: uint // ? kv ]
